            <ArticleTitle>Angiogenesis in myocardial laser &quot;revascularization&quot;.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>579-88</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The clinical and experimental data relevant to the theoretical mechanisms and clinical results of laser myocardial &quot;revascularization&quot; are reviewed. Both transmyocardial (TMR) and percutaneous (PMR) approaches are considered. Special attention is paid to the issue of TMR-induced angiogenesis. Both TMR and PMR result in a reduction in angina symptoms in patients refractory to conventional therapy and are likely to act through common pathways. TMR-induced angiogenesis appears to result from the up-regulation of vascular growth factors. However, the available data suggest that the angiogenic response is not a unique consequence of laser revascularization. Rather, the angiogenesis associated with TMR is likely to be a non-specific response of the myocardium to injury.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
